ange","author":"Jon Godwin","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6604","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6604"}},{"id":"6605","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4432","description":"From about 4'to about 6'there is a small hole with eddy service on the river left bank and a small wave just upstream to river right of the hole. You can surf back and forth between them. It all get's MUCH better above 7.5'. ","caption":"You should have seen it yesterday","poi_name":"","subject":"Stephen Strange","author":"Jon Godwin","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6605","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6605"}},{"id":"877289","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877514","description":"","caption":"Zack Miller Loop at Bunny Hole","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Mkeeney","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877289","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877289"}},{"id":"877287","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877512","description":"Less than 5 ft","caption":"Loop at Bunny Hole","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Mkeeney","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877287","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877287"}},{"id":"877290","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877515","description":"Normally there is a pillow on the front side of this rock, but that day there was a log broached on it.  Made for a somewhat sketchy surf in the hole above it!","caption":"Log broached at Little Ocoee Rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Mkeeney","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877290","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877290"}},{"id":"9672","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8077","description":"","caption":"Put In Bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"Wes & Knut","author":"John Grimes","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9672","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9672"}},{"id":"6602","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4432","description":"","caption":"Rockless Splat","poi_name":"","subject":"Jon Godwin","author":"Stephen Strange","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6602","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6602"}},{"id":"6603","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4432","description":"","caption":"Focused on the Flat","poi_name":"","subject":"Jon Godwin","author":"Stephen Strange","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6603","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6603"}},{"id":"9674","ext":"mov","post_id":"8078","description":"","caption":"Surfing the big wave","poi_name":"??","subject":"Craig MacKinnon","author":"Bridget Nudi","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9674","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9674"}},{"id":"9673","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8077","description":"The Master at work!","caption":"The Big Wave","poi_name":"","subject":"Craig MacKinnon","author":"John Grimes","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9673","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9673"}}],"detail":{"id":"1073","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"User Comments\n\n**Update on Put in Access at State Forest Rd. The NC State Forest requires a permit to access at the low bridge put in. Price has increased to $60/year or $13/trip for a kayak specific permit. We were not ticketed, but did receive a warning from the sheriff's department on 7/13/2024 when we paddled for an early morning run. The pass can be purchased by selecting 'fishing permit' or 'Kayak day pass' on the website: [Outdoor Access - G.W. Hill Forest Recreational Use Permits](https://app.outdooraccess.com/#/listing/16084). Attempts are being made to lower this price, which has increased over three times what it was years before and this will be updated if there is any success.**\n\nA pretty little run with lots of little rapids and some III's thrown in. Even at lower volumes the water keeps moving nicely until you reach the small broken out dam. From that point on, it slows considerably and becomes flat (pun intended) as you near the mouth and enter Lake Michie.\n\nRobert Martin 10-16-2000 12:38:04\n\nMinimum 2' Maximum (none). This is a beautiful paddle through NCSU forest land. At levels of 3'usgs and up there is a great surfing wave 2/3 through the run. Best play level is between 3' and 4'(IMHO)\n\n**Directions to Take Out**\n\nFrom Rt. 70 Bypass / Rt. 85 in Durham, take Exit 176B and go north on Duke St. about 2 1/2 miles to where it merges and becomes Roxboro St. Continue on Roxboro St. for about another 6 1/2 miles (9 miles all together). Turn right onto Bahama Rd. (watch for signs for the town of Bahama and Lake Michie and a traffic light at intersection). Go east on Bahama Rd. 3.3 miles, cross over the lake and turn right into Lake Michie Boat House area (there is parking on the left after the bridge up Wilkins Rd., but I've seen signs there saying they want all boat launches from the Boat House).\n\n**Directions from Take Out to Put In**\n\nTurn left out of Boat House area and go 1.4 miles west on Bahama Rd. Turn right onto Quail Roost Rd. Go about 2 1/2 miles to it's end at Highway 501(Roxboro Rd.). Turn right onto Highway 501 and take first right onto Moore's Mill Rd. Go 1/10 mile to State Forest Rd. (first road on right - after dirt driveway). Turn right onto State Forest Rd. (immediately turns to gravel). State Forest Rd. is not marked, but there is a hand stenciled sign saying 'Hill Forest' on the corner. There is a wooden 1-lane bridge crossing the Flat River about a mile down the road. The road is very narrow and there are no real shoulders. Parking is best on the far side of the bridge where the road widens a bit. The surrounding land is the property of NC State University and they have several access gates just off the road. Make sure you do not block these gates when you park. Access to the river is easy from either side of the bridge.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"3.8","correlations":[{"reachID":"1073","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"2","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"7"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"FLAT RIVER AT BAHAMA, NC","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02085500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"40","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"1.45","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"36.18277778","longitude":"-78.87888889"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02085500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02085500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1.45","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qu